    {
      "id": "zh-en-shopping",
      "title": "Shopping",
      "description": "Money, prices, and getting what you want",
      "theme": "food",
      "guide": {
        "intro": "Real Chinese shopping is fast and friendly. Three phrases get you 80% of the way there: 多少钱 (how much), 太贵了 (too expensive), and 给我 (give me).",
        "sections": [
          { "kind": "heading", "text": "Money words" },
          {
            "kind": "paragraph",
            "text": "Spoken Chinese uses 块 (kuài) for the main currency unit; written and formal Chinese uses 元 (yuán). They're the same value — like \"buck\" vs \"dollar\". 毛 (máo) and 分 (fēn) are tenths and hundredths."
          },
          {
            "kind": "table",
            "headers": ["Spoken", "Written", "Value"],
            "rows": [
              ["块 (kuài)", "元 (yuán)", "main unit"],
              ["毛 (máo)", "角 (jiǎo)", "1/10 of 块"],
              ["分 (fēn)", "分 (fēn)", "1/100 of 块"]
            ]
          },
          { "kind": "heading", "text": "Asking the price" },
          {
            "kind": "examples",
            "rows": [
              { "source": "多少 钱？", "pinyin": "Duō shǎo qián?", "translation": "How much (money)?" },
              { "source": "这个 多少 钱？", "pinyin": "Zhè ge duō shǎo qián?", "translation": "How much is this one?" },
              { "source": "十块。", "pinyin": "Shí kuài.", "translation": "Ten yuan." },
              { "source": "太贵了！", "pinyin": "Tài guì le!", "translation": "Too expensive!" }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
